00:05:02.930 --> 00:05:06.200
Next, on the connect pillar,
let's talk about VPC.

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:08.120
First of all, what is VPC?

00:05:08.120 --> 00:05:10.880
VPC stands for
virtual private cloud.

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:14.360
It is your own piece of
logically isolated and secure

00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:15.590
Google Cloud.

00:05:15.590 --> 00:05:17.580
It resembles the
traditional network,

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:20.432
but with the benefits of
scalable infrastructure.

00:05:20.432 --> 00:05:21.890
Now, what's unique
about our global

00:05:21.890 --> 00:05:24.650
VPC is that it uses
the same backbone

00:05:24.650 --> 00:05:26.780
that we have built
for our own products,

00:05:26.780 --> 00:05:29.690
like Google Search, YouTube,
that you saw earlier.

00:05:29.690 --> 00:05:33.080
And that enables our
customers to create VMs

00:05:33.080 --> 00:05:36.320
anywhere in the world,
across any of our 19 regions,

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:39.630
and have private connectivity
between them by default.

00:05:39.630 --> 00:05:41.480
So the customers
don't need to set up

00:05:41.480 --> 00:05:44.180
any VPN connections,
or peering connections,

00:05:44.180 --> 00:05:47.410
or give their VMs external IDs.

00:05:47.410 --> 00:05:50.890
This reduces the toil
of sprawl and config,

00:05:50.890 --> 00:05:53.920
and truly enables our
customers to have global access

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:55.030
to their workloads.

00:05:55.030 --> 00:05:59.100
And that's what we mean when
we say 19 regions, one VPC.

00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:02.830
Now on VPC, we have announced
two key advancements

00:06:02.830 --> 00:06:03.720
in the last one year.

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:07.270
The first one is private
access for GCP services.

00:06:07.270 --> 00:06:10.690
This is a new framework that
provides private connectivity

00:06:10.690 --> 00:06:13.660
between your VPC
network and the service

00:06:13.660 --> 00:06:15.370
that you're communicating to.

00:06:15.370 --> 00:06:19.450
Cloud SQL is the force GCP
service to use this framework.

00:06:19.450 --> 00:06:21.790
The VM instances
in your VPC network

00:06:21.790 --> 00:06:24.070
and the service
that you are talking

00:06:24.070 --> 00:06:28.720
to communicate via the
internal RFC 1918 IP addresses.

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:30.850
And once you set up
this connectivity,

00:06:30.850 --> 00:06:34.070
any number of GCP services
can use that connection.

00:06:34.070 --> 00:06:36.370
So again, to
summarize, it reduces

00:06:36.370 --> 00:06:41.320
these toil of management and
set up to talk to GCP services.

00:06:41.320 --> 00:06:44.430
The second advancement that
we have made on the VPC side

00:06:44.430 --> 00:06:46.920
is the private Google
access for on-prem.

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:49.890
This enables your
on-prem hosts to reach

00:06:49.890 --> 00:06:54.270
Google APIs and services through
a cloud VPN tunnel, or a Cloud

00:06:54.270 --> 00:06:55.650
Interconnect connection.

00:06:55.650 --> 00:06:58.650
So you can leverage your
existing private connection

00:06:58.650 --> 00:07:01.230
to Google Cloud for
this, and that also

00:07:01.230 --> 00:07:03.780
means that you can take
advantage of VPC service

00:07:03.780 --> 00:07:06.870
controls that Zach
will cover later.

00:07:06.870 --> 00:07:08.970
It does not require
your on-prem hosts

00:07:08.970 --> 00:07:12.640
to have external IP addresses.

00:07:12.640 --> 00:07:14.260
Next on the Connect
pillar, I want

00:07:14.260 --> 00:07:17.110
to talk about the Cloud DNS.

00:07:17.110 --> 00:07:19.420
We have added two
capabilities on Cloud DNS

00:07:19.420 --> 00:07:20.770
in the last one year.

00:07:20.770 --> 00:07:22.780
First one is private
zones, and the second one

00:07:22.780 --> 00:07:24.760
is DNS forwarding.

00:07:24.760 --> 00:07:28.360
Private zones provides a
simple to manage, internal DNS

00:07:28.360 --> 00:07:32.050
solution, for your
private networks on GCP.

00:07:32.050 --> 00:07:35.050
This removes basically
the need to provision

00:07:35.050 --> 00:07:37.360
and manage any
additional resources,

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:40.120
or compute resources,
simplifying management

00:07:40.120 --> 00:07:42.130
for networking administrators.

00:07:42.130 --> 00:07:44.360
Now, as you can see
in this animation,

00:07:44.360 --> 00:07:47.110
the DNS queries for private
zones, myenterprise.com,

00:07:47.110 --> 00:07:49.510
it'll just show
up, are restricted

00:07:49.510 --> 00:07:51.160
to your internal network.

00:07:51.160 --> 00:07:54.670
And any DNS queries will
that from the hostile agent

00:07:54.670 --> 00:07:56.230
on the right, from
public internet,

00:07:56.230 --> 00:08:00.700
get denied, because this
is a private network.

00:08:00.700 --> 00:08:03.510
Now, a key part of a successful
hybrid cloud strategy

00:08:03.510 --> 00:08:06.600
is that you should be able to
find your resources easily,

00:08:06.600 --> 00:08:09.640
whether they are
on-prem or on cloud.

00:08:09.640 --> 00:08:12.760
DNS forwarding lets you
link your on-prem and cloud

00:08:12.760 --> 00:08:14.650
environments easily.

00:08:14.650 --> 00:08:17.260
You can set up bi-directional
forwarding zones

00:08:17.260 --> 00:08:19.030
between your on-prem
name servers,

00:08:19.030 --> 00:08:21.836
and GCP's internal name service.

00:08:21.836 --> 00:08:26.110
As you can see in the animation,
on the outbound forwarding,

00:08:26.110 --> 00:08:30.130
it lets your GCP resources
use your existing on-prem DNS

00:08:30.130 --> 00:08:32.620
servers, and on the
inbound forwarding

00:08:32.620 --> 00:08:35.980
it lets you are on-prem
resources use the Cloud

00:08:35.980 --> 00:08:38.770
DNS to resolve domains.

00:08:38.770 --> 00:08:40.929
With that, we wrap up
the connect pillar,

00:08:40.929 --> 00:08:43.570
and we are now going
to move to optimize.

00:08:43.570 --> 00:08:46.660
The G8 network service
tiers years last month.

00:08:46.660 --> 00:08:49.280
Some of you may have seen that
announcement come through.

00:08:49.280 --> 00:08:51.880
Network service tiers
lets our customers

00:08:51.880 --> 00:08:54.200
customize the
underlying network,

00:08:54.200 --> 00:08:58.300
and basically optimize it
for performance or cost.

00:08:58.300 --> 00:09:00.550
So if you are using
Google Cloud today,

00:09:00.550 --> 00:09:03.740
you're most likely using
our premium tier network.

00:09:03.740 --> 00:09:05.650
The premium tier
delivers traffic

00:09:05.650 --> 00:09:09.310
over Google's well-provisioned,
low latency, highly reliable

00:09:09.310 --> 00:09:11.740
global network,
which we saw earlier.

00:09:11.740 --> 00:09:13.720
But for some key
cases, customers

00:09:13.720 --> 00:09:15.730
may prefer a
standard tier, which

00:09:15.730 --> 00:09:18.250
is a cheaper, lower
performance alternative,

00:09:18.250 --> 00:09:20.410
but it is still
comparable in performance

00:09:20.410 --> 00:09:22.300
to other public clouds.

00:09:22.300 --> 00:09:24.880
Most cloud providers use
what is called hot potato

00:09:24.880 --> 00:09:26.380
routing, which you
see on the bottom

00:09:26.380 --> 00:09:29.410
here, which means when
you try to send traffic

00:09:29.410 --> 00:09:31.510
on their network
it gets offloaded

00:09:31.510 --> 00:09:34.730
to the public internet
as soon as possible.

00:09:34.730 --> 00:09:36.670
Whereas, we use
cold potato routing,

00:09:36.670 --> 00:09:38.230
that you see on the top.

00:09:38.230 --> 00:09:40.810
We keep that traffic on
Google's private network

00:09:40.810 --> 00:09:43.480
for as long as
possible, and it usually

00:09:43.480 --> 00:09:46.750
exits the public internet as
close as possible to the end

00:09:46.750 --> 00:09:49.960
user, usually in the
same city or town.

00:09:49.960 --> 00:09:53.620
And that enables us to provide
better reliability, higher

00:09:53.620 --> 00:09:57.180
security, and
higher performance.

00:09:57.180 --> 00:10:00.370
Cedexis, an internet performance
monitoring and optimizations

00:10:00.370 --> 00:10:02.800
tools company, which
is now part of Citrix,

00:10:02.800 --> 00:10:04.990
did some performance
comparisons.

00:10:04.990 --> 00:10:10.000
And as expected, the premium
tier is 1.7x times faster than

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:11.380
the standard tier.

00:10:11.380 --> 00:10:14.200
So on the top is the throughput
from the premium tier

00:10:14.200 --> 00:10:17.890
in green, which is what you get
from our premium tier network.

00:10:17.890 --> 00:10:20.140
And on the bottom, in the
blue, is the standard deal

00:10:20.140 --> 00:10:22.840
throughput, which is what you
get from our standard deal,

00:10:22.840 --> 00:10:25.390
and most other public clouds.

00:10:25.390 --> 00:10:27.970
And you can get
these numbers online

00:10:27.970 --> 00:10:31.150
on the website of Cedexis.

00:10:31.150 --> 00:10:32.920
So that wraps up optimize.

00:10:32.920 --> 00:10:35.980
I would next like to move
into the scale pillar, which

00:10:35.980 --> 00:10:39.540
includes cloud load
balancing and cloud CDN.

00:10:39.540 --> 00:10:42.210
Before we talk about the
different advancements

00:10:42.210 --> 00:10:44.640
that we have made in
cloud load balancing,

00:10:44.640 --> 00:10:47.610
I would first like to talk about
how Google does global load

00:10:47.610 --> 00:10:49.350
balancing.

00:10:49.350 --> 00:10:52.880
Load balancers and most other
public clouds are regional,

00:10:52.880 --> 00:10:57.452
so that means you have a single
whip or virtual IP per region.

00:10:57.452 --> 00:10:59.660
And then you have backend
instances in those regions,

00:10:59.660 --> 00:11:00.800
as well.

00:11:00.800 --> 00:11:04.220
Now, regions are silos, so the
load balancer in one region

00:11:04.220 --> 00:11:08.240
can not use the capacity of
load balancer in another region.

00:11:08.240 --> 00:11:11.160
Whereas with Google
global load balancing,

00:11:11.160 --> 00:11:13.730
you can have your application
instances running anywhere

00:11:13.730 --> 00:11:17.000
in the world, across
any of our 19 regions,

00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:19.490
but you'll get a single
whip or virtual IP

00:11:19.490 --> 00:11:22.130
to frontend all those
backend instances.

00:11:22.130 --> 00:11:24.920
So, that way, the customers
get full global capacity

00:11:24.920 --> 00:11:27.260
and cross region fail over.

00:11:27.260 --> 00:11:29.840
It's also closely tied
to the autoscaler that

00:11:29.840 --> 00:11:32.600
scales up or down your
backend instances based

00:11:32.600 --> 00:11:34.200
on inline traffic.

00:11:34.200 --> 00:11:37.130
So, as you can see, this
again helps our customers

00:11:37.130 --> 00:11:40.430
deliver their services
closer to their end users,

00:11:40.430 --> 00:11:44.150
and scale up or down
in line with traffic.

00:11:44.150 --> 00:11:46.580
With that, let's take a look
at some of the advancements

00:11:46.580 --> 00:11:48.950
that we have made on
our load balancers.

00:11:48.950 --> 00:11:52.040
We offer five different
flavors of load balancers,

00:11:52.040 --> 00:11:53.540
and there are a
lot of capabilities

00:11:53.540 --> 00:11:56.480
that we have announced across
these different flavors

00:11:56.480 --> 00:11:58.040
over the last one year.

00:11:58.040 --> 00:12:00.350
I won't have the time to
go cover all of those.

00:12:00.350 --> 00:12:03.530
There is a deep dive session
on our load balanceres tomorrow

00:12:03.530 --> 00:12:05.430
if you are interested
in more details.

00:12:05.430 --> 00:12:07.820
I have just
handpicked a few here.

00:12:07.820 --> 00:12:09.890
On our internal load
balancer, we now

00:12:09.890 --> 00:12:12.860
support all ports, which
basically takes away

00:12:12.860 --> 00:12:15.470
the earlier requirement
of five ports.

00:12:15.470 --> 00:12:18.410
Very useful for users who
want to manage multiple layers

00:12:18.410 --> 00:12:20.540
for applications.

00:12:20.540 --> 00:12:23.000
Also the internal
load balancer now

00:12:23.000 --> 00:12:26.720
reduces the management
and the config setup,

00:12:26.720 --> 00:12:29.870
by supporting DNS based
service discovery.

00:12:29.870 --> 00:12:31.910
On the HTTP(S) load
balancer front,

00:12:31.910 --> 00:12:34.910
we now support user defined
request headers, which

00:12:34.910 --> 00:12:38.540
basically insert client
information, the geolocation,

00:12:38.540 --> 00:12:42.680
or the RTT, and then send
it to the backend instances.

00:12:42.680 --> 00:12:45.650
And lastly, in order to
continue our commitment

00:12:45.650 --> 00:12:49.220
of making the internet faster,
on the HTTP(S) load balancer,

00:12:49.220 --> 00:12:52.860
we support the quick and
the HTTP/2 protocols.

00:12:52.860 --> 00:12:55.680
So if your applications
are latency sensitive,

00:12:55.680 --> 00:12:57.530
they're going to see
a boost of performance

00:12:57.530 --> 00:13:00.380
as a result of that integration.

00:13:00.380 --> 00:13:02.270
On the Google Cloud
CDM, last year

00:13:02.270 --> 00:13:04.970
we went [INAUDIBLE] with
large object support,

00:13:04.970 --> 00:13:07.610
allowing you to cache and
serve video and other content

00:13:07.610 --> 00:13:11.310
up to five terabytes in size.

00:13:11.310 --> 00:13:15.570
So with that, I talked about
connect, optimize, and scale

00:13:15.570 --> 00:13:16.400
pillars.

00:13:16.400 --> 00:13:18.110
And I would like to
invite Zach to talk

00:13:18.110 --> 00:13:20.240
about secure and
modernized pillars next.

00:13:26.597 --> 00:13:27.430
ZACH SELIS: Rebecca.

00:13:27.430 --> 00:13:29.212
Let's jump right into
the secure pillar.

00:13:29.212 --> 00:13:31.420
We have a number of key
advancements over the last 12

00:13:31.420 --> 00:13:33.710
months that I want
to cover today.

00:13:33.710 --> 00:13:36.550
So historically, when you wanted
to store your sensitive data

00:13:36.550 --> 00:13:38.740
inside of a Google
Cloud managed service,

00:13:38.740 --> 00:13:40.570
you had a set of
credentials that

00:13:40.570 --> 00:13:42.970
were tied to an IAM role,
that actually determined

00:13:42.970 --> 00:13:44.898
whether or not a user
could access that data.

00:13:44.898 --> 00:13:46.690
So for example, if
you're storing your data

00:13:46.690 --> 00:13:49.030
in Google Cloud
Storage, access control

00:13:49.030 --> 00:13:52.300
is merely dictated by which
roles a particular user

00:13:52.300 --> 00:13:54.160
or a set of credentials have.

00:13:54.160 --> 00:13:55.840
This could open up
your organization

00:13:55.840 --> 00:13:57.320
to some weaknesses.

00:13:57.320 --> 00:13:59.860
For example, what if IAM
roles are misconfigured,

00:13:59.860 --> 00:14:02.290
providing more access
than you intended?

00:14:02.290 --> 00:14:04.640
What if those IAM
credentials are compromised?

00:14:04.640 --> 00:14:06.490
They are maybe leaked
publicly, or somehow

00:14:06.490 --> 00:14:07.990
get into the hands
of somebody who's

00:14:07.990 --> 00:14:10.092
not supposed to have them.

00:14:10.092 --> 00:14:11.800
And lastly, what if
you have an instance,

00:14:11.800 --> 00:14:14.230
a virtual machine inside of
your environment, that gets

00:14:14.230 --> 00:14:15.790
compromised, and
somebody leverages

00:14:15.790 --> 00:14:18.220
that compromised instance
to try and exfiltrate data

00:14:18.220 --> 00:14:20.930
from your VPC environment?

00:14:20.930 --> 00:14:23.420
In addition to this,
traditional network controls,

00:14:23.420 --> 00:14:25.780
so access lists that
are restricting access,

00:14:25.780 --> 00:14:28.270
based on IP addresses
and protocols,

00:14:28.270 --> 00:14:30.820
don't provide
complete protection,

00:14:30.820 --> 00:14:33.250
because most Google
services are exposed

00:14:33.250 --> 00:14:34.750
from an accessibility
perspective

00:14:34.750 --> 00:14:37.875
over the same infrastructure,
leveraging the same protocols.

00:14:37.875 --> 00:14:39.250
As you've experienced
when you're

00:14:39.250 --> 00:14:42.130
accessing our APIs across a
number of Google products,

00:14:42.130 --> 00:14:45.530
it's almost always TLS
encrypted HTTP traffic.

00:14:45.530 --> 00:14:47.710
So just relying on
the network controls

00:14:47.710 --> 00:14:50.680
themselves didn't provide
enough protections.

00:14:50.680 --> 00:14:54.053
To solve this problem, we
developed VPC Service Controls.

00:14:54.053 --> 00:14:54.970
This is now available.

00:14:54.970 --> 00:14:56.980
It was released in
the last 12 months.

00:14:56.980 --> 00:14:59.290
VPC Service Controls
provide you with the ability

00:14:59.290 --> 00:15:04.600
to actually permit or deny,
on a per project basis,

00:15:04.600 --> 00:15:08.110
access to Google managed
service APIs, based

00:15:08.110 --> 00:15:10.730
on the origin of the request.

00:15:10.730 --> 00:15:13.330
So this, in combination
with the proper credentials,

00:15:13.330 --> 00:15:16.390
and the network access controls,
allow you to basically define

00:15:16.390 --> 00:15:19.420
an explicit security
perimeter around your projects

00:15:19.420 --> 00:15:21.670
and networks inside
of Google Cloud,

00:15:21.670 --> 00:15:24.140
across which data
cannot transfer.

00:15:24.140 --> 00:15:26.230
So for example, if
your intended policy

00:15:26.230 --> 00:15:29.170
is that you want to create a
Google Cloud Storage bucket,

00:15:29.170 --> 00:15:31.812
and store your data in that
bucket inside of a project,

00:15:31.812 --> 00:15:34.270
but you don't want to allow
access from the public internet

00:15:34.270 --> 00:15:38.062
to that bucket, you can do
that with VPC service controls,

00:15:38.062 --> 00:15:39.520
even if those users
on the internet

00:15:39.520 --> 00:15:41.810
have the right credentials.

00:15:41.810 --> 00:15:44.650
So VPC service controls
is very powerful.

00:15:44.650 --> 00:15:47.260
Provides you with
API level controls

00:15:47.260 --> 00:15:50.320
to add the origin of the
request to the existing

00:15:50.320 --> 00:15:52.120
suite of controls,
to control access

00:15:52.120 --> 00:15:57.100
to data that you store inside
of Google's managed services.

00:15:57.100 --> 00:15:59.320
Next we have Cloud
Armor Cloud Armor

00:15:59.320 --> 00:16:02.440
allows you to protect
your internet facing,

00:16:02.440 --> 00:16:05.380
load balanced workloads,
inside of Google Cloud.

00:16:05.380 --> 00:16:08.110
This includes protecting you
from a variety of distributed

00:16:08.110 --> 00:16:10.050
denial of service attacks.

00:16:10.050 --> 00:16:11.800
And this leverages
the same protections

00:16:11.800 --> 00:16:14.410
that Google has developed
over the past 20 years

00:16:14.410 --> 00:16:16.480
to make sure that
our own services--

00:16:16.480 --> 00:16:18.850
Drive, Gmail,
Search, et cetera--

00:16:18.850 --> 00:16:21.940
remain available and
scalable on a global basis.

00:16:21.940 --> 00:16:24.760
Cloud Armor is integrated with
Google Cloud load balancing,

00:16:24.760 --> 00:16:27.160
and provides you the ability
to implement these controls

00:16:27.160 --> 00:16:28.750
at the edge of Google's network.

00:16:28.750 --> 00:16:31.270
So when Shubhikia talked
about our global backbone

00:16:31.270 --> 00:16:33.760
and leveraging our premium
network where your users come

00:16:33.760 --> 00:16:36.970
on to Google's network as
close to the user as possible,

00:16:36.970 --> 00:16:40.090
Cloud Armor allows you to
implement controls to determine

00:16:40.090 --> 00:16:42.810
from both layer seven--

00:16:42.810 --> 00:16:44.560
sorry, layer three,
so traditional network

00:16:44.560 --> 00:16:47.020
controls, all the way
through Layer seven,

00:16:47.020 --> 00:16:50.330
what users are able to access
your load balanced workloads.

00:16:50.330 --> 00:16:54.410
So Cloud Armor is available
now publicly as well.

00:16:54.410 --> 00:16:55.810
Next, we have Cloud NAT.

00:16:55.810 --> 00:16:58.090
This was probably one of the
most frequently requested

00:16:58.090 --> 00:17:00.507
capabilities from customers,
and we've made this available

00:17:00.507 --> 00:17:02.650
now in the last 12 months.

00:17:02.650 --> 00:17:05.230
Traditionally when you deploy
resources, applications,

00:17:05.230 --> 00:17:07.480
virtual machines
inside of the cloud,

00:17:07.480 --> 00:17:10.089
you want to protect those
systems from direct access

00:17:10.089 --> 00:17:13.800
from the public internet
at the same time.

00:17:13.800 --> 00:17:14.300
Yep.

00:17:14.300 --> 00:17:17.200
So Cloud NAT solves
this problem for you

00:17:17.200 --> 00:17:19.130
by implementing a
regional cloud NAT

00:17:19.130 --> 00:17:21.520
gateway that allows
you to control access,

00:17:21.520 --> 00:17:24.220
outbound access, from
those virtual machines

00:17:24.220 --> 00:17:27.220
or your applications, in an
efficient manner on a per

00:17:27.220 --> 00:17:28.590
region basis.

00:17:28.590 --> 00:17:31.390
There are a number of key
benefits to our Cloud NAT

00:17:31.390 --> 00:17:32.840
implementation.

00:17:32.840 --> 00:17:35.560
First, it's a completely
software driven, distributed

00:17:35.560 --> 00:17:36.550
implementation.

00:17:36.550 --> 00:17:38.200
So the actual NAT
capabilities are

00:17:38.200 --> 00:17:41.560
built into the fabric
of the VPC itself.

00:17:41.560 --> 00:17:43.000
So there's no
device that you have

00:17:43.000 --> 00:17:45.340
to force traffic through
from an app perspective.

00:17:45.340 --> 00:17:47.950
There's no risk of potential
choke points from a performance

00:17:47.950 --> 00:17:51.520
perspective, because it's a
completely distributed system.

00:17:51.520 --> 00:17:55.030
Second, we give you the control
over whether Google manages

00:17:55.030 --> 00:17:57.970
the public IP addresses, as you
scale up and down the number

00:17:57.970 --> 00:18:00.550
of translations that need to
happen for traffic destined

00:18:00.550 --> 00:18:02.890
to the internet, or
whether you explicitly

00:18:02.890 --> 00:18:05.620
define through static
IP reservations

00:18:05.620 --> 00:18:08.680
in your projects, which public
IP addresses are associated

00:18:08.680 --> 00:18:09.573
with NAT.

00:18:09.573 --> 00:18:10.990
The latter case
being very common,

00:18:10.990 --> 00:18:12.448
for example, if
you're establishing

00:18:12.448 --> 00:18:14.192
outbound connections
to partners,

00:18:14.192 --> 00:18:16.150
and those partners need
to whitelist the source

00:18:16.150 --> 00:18:17.987
IP addresses that
you're coming from.

00:18:17.987 --> 00:18:19.570
You have a configuration
choice there.

00:18:19.570 --> 00:18:21.640
Google can manage those
addresses for you,

00:18:21.640 --> 00:18:24.100
or you can specify through
static reservations which

00:18:24.100 --> 00:18:26.710
public IP addresses your
services present themselves

00:18:26.710 --> 00:18:29.510
as on the public internet.

00:18:29.510 --> 00:18:32.710
And finally, a single
cloud NAT gateway

00:18:32.710 --> 00:18:36.470
can support an entire
region, regardless of scale.

00:18:36.470 --> 00:18:38.590
So if you have 100 virtual
machines in a region

00:18:38.590 --> 00:18:41.500
if you have 15,000 virtual
machines in a region,

00:18:41.500 --> 00:18:43.660
irrespective of
the number of VMs,

00:18:43.660 --> 00:18:45.317
irrespective of the
volume of traffic

00:18:45.317 --> 00:18:46.900
that's going out
towards the internet,

00:18:46.900 --> 00:18:49.510
from that particular region you
only need a single cloud NAT

00:18:49.510 --> 00:18:50.220
gateway.

00:18:50.220 --> 00:18:51.970
And again this goes
back to the uniqueness

00:18:51.970 --> 00:18:54.345
and the architecture, and the
distributed way in which we

00:18:54.345 --> 00:18:56.290
implement these capabilities.

00:18:56.290 --> 00:18:59.655
Cloud NAT is the third key
capability in the secure pillar

00:18:59.655 --> 00:19:01.030
that we want to
talk about today.

00:19:04.570 --> 00:19:07.150
Next we have Google-managed
certificates.

00:19:07.150 --> 00:19:10.510
So Google has a history of
leveraging TLS encryption

00:19:10.510 --> 00:19:12.183
whenever possible.

00:19:12.183 --> 00:19:13.600
If you remember
several years ago,

00:19:13.600 --> 00:19:15.850
we actually let the
ability for websites

00:19:15.850 --> 00:19:18.940
who are using HTTPS to
improve their search result

00:19:18.940 --> 00:19:19.900
rankings, right?

00:19:19.900 --> 00:19:22.630
So you're using encrypted
transport on the internet.

00:19:22.630 --> 00:19:25.600
That actually influences
search page results.

00:19:25.600 --> 00:19:28.690
At the same time, we also made
the Chrome browser explicitly

00:19:28.690 --> 00:19:32.530
kind of identify when websites
were not using TLS, changing

00:19:32.530 --> 00:19:34.150
the standard
expectation of users

00:19:34.150 --> 00:19:36.250
to assume that all
websites should be using

00:19:36.250 --> 00:19:39.820
TLS encryption, and visually,
actually identifying,

00:19:39.820 --> 00:19:40.960
when websites do not.

00:19:40.960 --> 00:19:43.622
So really kind of changing
the approach there.

00:19:43.622 --> 00:19:45.580
At the same time within
Cloud, We're constantly

00:19:45.580 --> 00:19:49.150
trying to make things easier for
customers to deploy and manage,

00:19:49.150 --> 00:19:51.610
and for this we have made
available in the last year

00:19:51.610 --> 00:19:53.650
Google-managed certificates.

00:19:53.650 --> 00:19:55.150
So with Google-managed
certificates,

00:19:55.150 --> 00:19:58.750
when you create an internet
facing HTTPS load balancer,

00:19:58.750 --> 00:20:00.250
as part of that
provisioning you can

00:20:00.250 --> 00:20:03.100
select to have Google
automatically procure

00:20:03.100 --> 00:20:05.108
and configure the
certificate for the domains

00:20:05.108 --> 00:20:07.150
that are supporting that
particular load balancer

00:20:07.150 --> 00:20:08.500
instance.

00:20:08.500 --> 00:20:11.020
In addition, this
capability automatically

00:20:11.020 --> 00:20:14.320
handles certificate renewal
when required, and will

00:20:14.320 --> 00:20:16.720
automatically revoke
the certificates when

00:20:16.720 --> 00:20:18.280
you delete the load balancer.

00:20:18.280 --> 00:20:21.130
So this now allows you to
basically have complete hands

00:20:21.130 --> 00:20:23.350
off TLS implementation
for your internet

00:20:23.350 --> 00:20:26.620
facing load balancers through
Google-managed certificates.

00:20:26.620 --> 00:20:28.900
In addition, regardless of
how you host your backends,

00:20:28.900 --> 00:20:32.530
whether those are on
compute instance VMs,

00:20:32.530 --> 00:20:36.190
or backed from a workload
perspective on Kubernetes pods,

00:20:36.190 --> 00:20:39.170
both types are supported
with this capability.

00:20:39.170 --> 00:20:41.710
So really making kind of
the default stance of TLS

00:20:41.710 --> 00:20:44.620
encrypted, load balancing,
very easy for you

00:20:44.620 --> 00:20:48.040
to manage and deploy.

00:20:48.040 --> 00:20:50.030
The next two capabilities
fall under the area

00:20:50.030 --> 00:20:52.180
we consider to be
network telemetry.

00:20:52.180 --> 00:20:54.620
And the first one is
called VPC flow logs.

00:20:54.620 --> 00:20:57.080
So having visibility,
obviously, to traffic inside

00:20:57.080 --> 00:20:59.860
of the cloud network, is a
common request of customers.

00:20:59.860 --> 00:21:02.360
This is something that I think
customers have kind of become

00:21:02.360 --> 00:21:05.190
accustomed to in their on
premise network environments.

00:21:05.190 --> 00:21:08.090
And so for traffic that's
traversing your VPC

00:21:08.090 --> 00:21:11.960
inside of Google Cloud, whether
it stays solely within the VPC,

00:21:11.960 --> 00:21:14.300
say between different virtual
machines inside of Google

00:21:14.300 --> 00:21:16.820
Cloud, or whether it's virtual
machines that are talking

00:21:16.820 --> 00:21:19.100
to resources out on
the public internet,

00:21:19.100 --> 00:21:23.000
you can now enable VPC flow logs
to get identifying information

00:21:23.000 --> 00:21:25.910
about that traffic
streamed and to sack driver

00:21:25.910 --> 00:21:27.830
from a visibility perspective.

00:21:27.830 --> 00:21:29.580
So if you have a
background in networking,

00:21:29.580 --> 00:21:31.610
this is pretty
analogous to NetFlow

00:21:31.610 --> 00:21:34.250
from your on-prem networking,
with some really great

00:21:34.250 --> 00:21:35.690
additional features.

00:21:35.690 --> 00:21:38.300
Namely that, not only do we
actually sample the traffic

00:21:38.300 --> 00:21:41.270
to determine the
traditional network

00:21:41.270 --> 00:21:42.690
information from that traffic--

00:21:42.690 --> 00:21:45.500
so source and destination
IP addresses, what protocols

00:21:45.500 --> 00:21:47.270
are used, what ports--

00:21:47.270 --> 00:21:49.400
we then annotate that
information with more,

00:21:49.400 --> 00:21:51.140
I'll say, human
friendly information.

00:21:51.140 --> 00:21:53.330
So we add the
project information,

00:21:53.330 --> 00:21:56.690
the actual virtual machine
instance names, the VPC

00:21:56.690 --> 00:21:58.670
that that instance
is deployed in,

00:21:58.670 --> 00:22:00.170
and if the communication
is actually

00:22:00.170 --> 00:22:02.060
from an instance
of virtual machine

00:22:02.060 --> 00:22:04.590
inside of your VPC to
the public internet,

00:22:04.590 --> 00:22:06.590
we ll annotate those
logs to tell you

00:22:06.590 --> 00:22:09.020
the geographic information of
what they're talking to so.

00:22:09.020 --> 00:22:10.437
You're talking to
an endpoint that

00:22:10.437 --> 00:22:12.980
may be in Sao Paolo,
Brazil, as an example.

00:22:12.980 --> 00:22:15.380
So these annotations
allow us to basically add

00:22:15.380 --> 00:22:18.320
additional information
that we know about the flow

00:22:18.320 --> 00:22:20.090
to those flow records.

00:22:20.090 --> 00:22:22.220
This information is
streamed natively by default

00:22:22.220 --> 00:22:23.810
to stack Stackdriver Logging.

00:22:23.810 --> 00:22:26.690
You can also stream it into
BigQuery for analytics.

00:22:26.690 --> 00:22:29.750
Or if you're using your own
on premise or external logging

00:22:29.750 --> 00:22:32.510
analytics capabilities, you
can actually stream the logs

00:22:32.510 --> 00:22:35.360
to those through Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:22:35.360 --> 00:22:38.090
So pretty powerful
capability here.

00:22:38.090 --> 00:22:41.330
In a similar fashion, we've also
released in the last 12 months

00:22:41.330 --> 00:22:44.180
logging for native VPC firewall.

00:22:44.180 --> 00:22:47.990
So you can now enable on a
per VPC firewall rule basis

00:22:47.990 --> 00:22:49.550
the ability to enable logging.

00:22:49.550 --> 00:22:51.590
So as traffic comes
through the VPC

00:22:51.590 --> 00:22:53.660
and matches these
firewall rules,

00:22:53.660 --> 00:22:55.610
we'll generate logs in
a very similar manner

00:22:55.610 --> 00:22:57.483
that we do for VPC flow logging.

00:22:57.483 --> 00:22:59.900
So you now have the ability
from a compliance perspective,

00:22:59.900 --> 00:23:02.138
as an example, to
actually prove to auditors

00:23:02.138 --> 00:23:03.680
that you're blocking
traffic that you

00:23:03.680 --> 00:23:05.330
say you're going to block.

00:23:05.330 --> 00:23:08.540
This is like VPC flow
logs, available natively

00:23:08.540 --> 00:23:10.790
for streaming into stack
Stackdriver Logging.

00:23:10.790 --> 00:23:13.640
Again also with BigQuery,
or two external systems

00:23:13.640 --> 00:23:15.470
through Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:23:15.470 --> 00:23:16.970
So two things with
network telemetry

00:23:16.970 --> 00:23:19.310
to give you better visibility
into the cloud network

00:23:19.310 --> 00:23:22.650
environment for both traffic
flowing across your VPC,

00:23:22.650 --> 00:23:25.700
but also for traffic flowing
to the native VPC firewall.

00:23:28.540 --> 00:23:30.670
So those are some of
the key capabilities

00:23:30.670 --> 00:23:31.840
from a secure perspective.

00:23:31.840 --> 00:23:33.498
Now moving on to modernize.

00:23:33.498 --> 00:23:36.040
We're going to talk specifically
here about Google Kubernetes

00:23:36.040 --> 00:23:39.250
Engine, and some stuff that
we're doing around Istio.

00:23:39.250 --> 00:23:41.890
So Google Kubernetes Engine,
if you're not familiar,

00:23:41.890 --> 00:23:45.370
is Google's managed
instance of Kubernetes,

00:23:45.370 --> 00:23:48.310
which is our production
ready solution for deploying

00:23:48.310 --> 00:23:50.128
containerized applications.

00:23:50.128 --> 00:23:51.670
Over the past year,
we've implemented

00:23:51.670 --> 00:23:53.128
a number of
capabilities that allow

00:23:53.128 --> 00:23:57.640
us to more tightly integrate GKE
clusters with the best of cloud

00:23:57.640 --> 00:24:00.125
native networking
inside of Google Cloud.

00:24:00.125 --> 00:24:02.500
So the power that we have in
terms of global reach around

00:24:02.500 --> 00:24:05.680
our VPCs, as well as the native
high performance routing,

00:24:05.680 --> 00:24:08.890
that's now integrated directly
into Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:24:08.890 --> 00:24:12.010
And we do this through something
we call VPC native clusters,

00:24:12.010 --> 00:24:14.260
which basically takes the
networking that is typically

00:24:14.260 --> 00:24:17.440
implemented as an overlay
inside of Kubernetes,

00:24:17.440 --> 00:24:19.540
and actually makes
that a fundamental part

00:24:19.540 --> 00:24:21.950
of the underlying VPC network.

00:24:21.950 --> 00:24:23.800
So this is good not
only from a visibility

00:24:23.800 --> 00:24:26.540
and from a routing perspective,
because we now for example

00:24:26.540 --> 00:24:28.570
treat pods and
services the same way

00:24:28.570 --> 00:24:31.900
we treat virtual machines from
a reachability perspective.

00:24:31.900 --> 00:24:33.550
But it also improves
the security,

00:24:33.550 --> 00:24:36.010
where we have strong kind
of anti spoofing measures

00:24:36.010 --> 00:24:38.590
that are deployed inside of
the network, that are natively

00:24:38.590 --> 00:24:41.488
integrated inside of GKE.

00:24:41.488 --> 00:24:43.030
This provides
additional capabilities

00:24:43.030 --> 00:24:44.440
when we look at
integrating networking

00:24:44.440 --> 00:24:45.857
into your on premise
environments,

00:24:45.857 --> 00:24:47.320
or hybrid networking.

00:24:47.320 --> 00:24:50.350
Because again, capabilities that
are enabled as part of your VPC

00:24:50.350 --> 00:24:52.750
from a routing and
visibility perspective

00:24:52.750 --> 00:24:55.900
are also now available for
your Kubernetes clusters.

00:24:55.900 --> 00:24:58.750
So very powerful capabilities
with respect to GKE.

00:25:02.600 --> 00:25:04.550
Also for GKE we've
released something

00:25:04.550 --> 00:25:06.380
called Container-native
load balancing.

00:25:06.380 --> 00:25:08.720
This is currently in beta.

00:25:08.720 --> 00:25:12.303
So in the past, when a load
balancer, like an external load

00:25:12.303 --> 00:25:14.220
balancer, like our Google
Cloud load balancer,

00:25:14.220 --> 00:25:17.240
targeted resources inside
of a Kubernetes cluster,

00:25:17.240 --> 00:25:19.550
you'd have a situation somewhat
similar to this, right?

00:25:19.550 --> 00:25:23.020
You have multiple nodes inside
of your Kubernetes cluster.

00:25:23.020 --> 00:25:25.220
The load balancer actually
targets the nodes,

00:25:25.220 --> 00:25:26.870
or the virtual
machine instances,

00:25:26.870 --> 00:25:29.810
as the backends for those
for the load balancer.

00:25:29.810 --> 00:25:33.320
Now on those nodes, you may
have a disproportionate number

00:25:33.320 --> 00:25:35.060
of pods in Kubernetes.

00:25:35.060 --> 00:25:37.460
You could even have a situation
where some of those nodes

00:25:37.460 --> 00:25:39.200
don't actually have
the pods that you're

00:25:39.200 --> 00:25:41.930
targeting from the load
balancing perspective scheduled

00:25:41.930 --> 00:25:43.080
on that node.

00:25:43.080 --> 00:25:47.030
And in that case, IP tables, or
kube-proxy, on those Kubernetes

00:25:47.030 --> 00:25:50.653
nodes, was responsible for a
second level of load balancing.

00:25:50.653 --> 00:25:52.070
So the external
load balancer gets

00:25:52.070 --> 00:25:55.520
the traffic to the node, IP
tables, or kube-proxy inside

00:25:55.520 --> 00:25:57.530
of Kubernetes, is
responsible for then getting

00:25:57.530 --> 00:25:59.420
the traffic to the
direct pod that you're

00:25:59.420 --> 00:26:00.847
trying to communicate with.

00:26:00.847 --> 00:26:02.930
This is commonly known in
the Kubernetes community

00:26:02.930 --> 00:26:04.250
as a double hop problem.

00:26:04.250 --> 00:26:07.130
You basically have two
layers of load balancing.

00:26:07.130 --> 00:26:08.900
This is especially
problematic if you

00:26:08.900 --> 00:26:11.570
look at something like a
regional Kubernetes cluster,

00:26:11.570 --> 00:26:14.480
where you have the actual nodes
for the GKE cluster spread

00:26:14.480 --> 00:26:17.060
across multiple zones
inside the region.

00:26:17.060 --> 00:26:19.220
The external load balancer
could send that traffic

00:26:19.220 --> 00:26:22.230
to a node in zone
A, as an example.

00:26:22.230 --> 00:26:24.335
And IP tables in
that node in zone A

00:26:24.335 --> 00:26:26.210
could then decide it's
going to actually load

00:26:26.210 --> 00:26:27.890
balanced that traffic
to a pod that's

00:26:27.890 --> 00:26:32.217
running on a node inside of
Zone B, or C, or D, right?

00:26:32.217 --> 00:26:33.800
So that second layer
of load balancing

00:26:33.800 --> 00:26:37.310
can add not only higher latency,
but for a regional cluster

00:26:37.310 --> 00:26:39.130
it can add additional
costs, because you're

00:26:39.130 --> 00:26:41.630
paying for the traffic that
goes between the different zones

00:26:41.630 --> 00:26:43.430
inside of a region.

00:26:43.430 --> 00:26:46.020
So now with container native
load balancing we've internet.

00:26:46.020 --> 00:26:48.447
We've introduced something
called network endpoint groups.

00:26:48.447 --> 00:26:50.030
And essentially here,
what we're doing

00:26:50.030 --> 00:26:52.250
is we're allowing the
pods to basically behave

00:26:52.250 --> 00:26:55.430
as if they're virtual machines
from a networking perspective,

00:26:55.430 --> 00:26:57.110
inside of the VPC.

00:26:57.110 --> 00:26:59.030
So instead of the
Kubernetes nodes actually

00:26:59.030 --> 00:27:01.130
being the target of
the load balancer,

00:27:01.130 --> 00:27:03.410
it's in fact the
pods themselves that

00:27:03.410 --> 00:27:06.200
are the direct target of
the external load balancer.

00:27:06.200 --> 00:27:08.030
So when traffic comes
in from your users

00:27:08.030 --> 00:27:10.580
to our external load
balancer, the load balancer

00:27:10.580 --> 00:27:13.040
looks at not the backends
from a node perspective

00:27:13.040 --> 00:27:14.370
inside of Kubernetes.

00:27:14.370 --> 00:27:17.075
It actually sends the traffic
directly to the Kubernetes

00:27:17.075 --> 00:27:18.630
pods themselves.

00:27:18.630 --> 00:27:21.590
So you remove essentially the
second half of load balancing.

00:27:21.590 --> 00:27:24.680
This improves latency and
can actually reduce costs

00:27:24.680 --> 00:27:28.820
for traffic going between zones
inside of a regional cluster.

00:27:28.820 --> 00:27:31.010
So container native load
balancing, very powerful

00:27:31.010 --> 00:27:32.760
as well.

00:27:32.760 --> 00:27:36.110
Also for GKE, we've actually
provided the ability now

00:27:36.110 --> 00:27:39.920
to integrate the load
balancing capabilities in GKE

00:27:39.920 --> 00:27:42.150
with some of our cloud
native capabilities,

00:27:42.150 --> 00:27:44.570
particularly with our content
delivery network, Cloud

00:27:44.570 --> 00:27:49.010
CDN, Identity Aware Proxy,
IAP, and Cloud Armor,

00:27:49.010 --> 00:27:51.500
which I talked about
a few slides ago.

00:27:51.500 --> 00:27:55.730
Now you can configure and
tune these capabilities

00:27:55.730 --> 00:27:58.580
directly from within Kubernetes
manifest or configuration

00:27:58.580 --> 00:27:59.660
files.

00:27:59.660 --> 00:28:03.250
And the way we do this is with
a concept called BackendConfig.

00:28:03.250 --> 00:28:05.000
You use BackendConfig
to configure

00:28:05.000 --> 00:28:11.090
these native services, so CDN,
IAP, Cloud Armor, et cetera.

00:28:11.090 --> 00:28:13.310
Independent of
Kubernetes ingress,

00:28:13.310 --> 00:28:16.610
which defines the web based
load balancer, or services.

00:28:16.610 --> 00:28:18.740
So you don't have to keep
overloading the existing

00:28:18.740 --> 00:28:21.530
Kubernetes constructs with
annotation and other things

00:28:21.530 --> 00:28:25.550
that need to be approved by the
broader Kubernetes community

00:28:25.550 --> 00:28:28.770
to integrate with Google
Cloud native capabilities.

00:28:28.770 --> 00:28:31.370
So with BackendConfig
you now have the ability

00:28:31.370 --> 00:28:34.760
to specify, independently
of ingress and services,

00:28:34.760 --> 00:28:37.820
integration with these
native Google Cloud Services.

00:28:37.820 --> 00:28:40.020
And then you pair those
two things together.

00:28:40.020 --> 00:28:43.190
So for example, an external web
based load balancer that you

00:28:43.190 --> 00:28:45.300
may have established,
you may instantiate it

00:28:45.300 --> 00:28:48.140
as a Kubernetes ingress, can
now also integrate natively

00:28:48.140 --> 00:28:52.670
with Cloud CDN, can now also
integrate natively with IAP,

00:28:52.670 --> 00:28:55.520
and can now also integrate
natively with Cloud Armor.

00:28:55.520 --> 00:29:00.670
So very powerful capabilities
from a Kubernetes perspective.

00:29:00.670 --> 00:29:01.870
Now moving on to Istio.

00:29:01.870 --> 00:29:03.370
So if you haven't
heard about Istio,

00:29:03.370 --> 00:29:05.200
which I imagine
most of you have,

00:29:05.200 --> 00:29:08.170
Istio is an open
source service mesh.

00:29:08.170 --> 00:29:11.260
It's heavily inspired by a lot
of internal systems at Google,

00:29:11.260 --> 00:29:13.300
but it is developed
in collaboration

00:29:13.300 --> 00:29:14.780
with a number of
external partners,

00:29:14.780 --> 00:29:18.340
including IBM and Lyft,
the ride sharing company,

00:29:18.340 --> 00:29:20.608
from a open source perspective.

00:29:20.608 --> 00:29:22.150
It essentially
provides a way for you

00:29:22.150 --> 00:29:26.140
to build a service mesh between
your different microservices

00:29:26.140 --> 00:29:28.000
that includes load
balancing, that

00:29:28.000 --> 00:29:30.670
includes very fine grained
telemetry and monitoring

00:29:30.670 --> 00:29:33.790
capabilities, and also allows
you to implement service

00:29:33.790 --> 00:29:35.780
to service network
based authentication.

00:29:35.780 --> 00:29:38.530
So mutual TLS
authentication, and allows

00:29:38.530 --> 00:29:40.780
you to do all of these
things transparently,

00:29:40.780 --> 00:29:43.360
without making any
code changes directly

00:29:43.360 --> 00:29:45.265
to your application code.

00:29:45.265 --> 00:29:47.140
So it essentially provides
an ability for you

00:29:47.140 --> 00:29:49.600
to abstract away all of
these common capabilities

00:29:49.600 --> 00:29:51.670
in the cloud environment,
without having

00:29:51.670 --> 00:29:54.220
to repetitively implement these
in terms of your application

00:29:54.220 --> 00:29:55.447
code.

00:29:55.447 --> 00:29:57.280
Now what we've released
in the last year is,

00:29:57.280 --> 00:30:00.820
you can now with a single click,
or a single flag in the CLI,

00:30:00.820 --> 00:30:04.030
you can actually provision
Istio as add-on on

00:30:04.030 --> 00:30:06.530
for your GKE clusters.

00:30:06.530 --> 00:30:08.000
When you do this,
what happens is,

00:30:08.000 --> 00:30:11.180
we automatically deploy
the Istio control plane.

00:30:11.180 --> 00:30:13.730
The Istio control plane is made
up of a number of services.

00:30:13.730 --> 00:30:16.040
Pilot, which handles the
actual traffic policies

00:30:16.040 --> 00:30:19.820
for which traffic goes where,
Mixer, which handles telemetry,

00:30:19.820 --> 00:30:22.790
the visibility we talked
about, and policy controls.

00:30:22.790 --> 00:30:25.100
And Citadel, which handles
automatic certificate

00:30:25.100 --> 00:30:27.050
provisioning and revocation.

00:30:27.050 --> 00:30:30.200
All these together are
deployed in your GKE cluster.

00:30:30.200 --> 00:30:32.360
And not only are they
deployed with one click,

00:30:32.360 --> 00:30:34.820
but for the ongoing upgrade
life of those control plane

00:30:34.820 --> 00:30:36.490
services, we
automatically handle

00:30:36.490 --> 00:30:38.190
the upgrades for those as well.

00:30:38.190 --> 00:30:42.590
This again is part of the
add-on capabilities with GKE.

00:30:42.590 --> 00:30:44.750
And with that, I'd
like to welcome up

00:30:44.750 --> 00:30:46.250
Nick Jacques from
Target, to talk

00:30:46.250 --> 00:30:49.140
about how Target is leveraging
some of these capabilities.

00:30:49.140 --> 00:30:51.960
[APPLAUSE]

00:30:53.850 --> 00:30:54.970
NICK JACQUES: Right.

00:30:54.970 --> 00:30:55.810
Thanks, Zach.

00:30:55.810 --> 00:30:57.200
Really glad to be here.

00:30:57.200 --> 00:30:58.330
My name is Nick Jacques.

00:30:58.330 --> 00:31:00.220
I am a lead engineer
at Target, and I work

00:31:00.220 --> 00:31:02.510
on our cloud platform team.

00:31:02.510 --> 00:31:04.900
My team is responsible
for building, managing,

00:31:04.900 --> 00:31:07.720
and operating the
infrastructure and the platform

00:31:07.720 --> 00:31:11.990
that our application teams
at Target deploy on top of.

00:31:11.990 --> 00:31:15.520
So if you're not familiar with
Target, just a brief refresher.

00:31:15.520 --> 00:31:17.300
We're a nationwide retailer.

00:31:17.300 --> 00:31:18.850
We have over 1,800 stores.

00:31:18.850 --> 00:31:22.270
At last count, I
think it's 1,845.

00:31:22.270 --> 00:31:26.170
39 distribution centers, and
over 350,000 team members.

00:31:26.170 --> 00:31:28.780
We have headquarters
locations across the US,

00:31:28.780 --> 00:31:31.900
but notably in Minneapolis,
and in the Bay Area here.

00:31:31.900 --> 00:31:36.880
And we currently rank number
39 on the Fortune 500.

00:31:36.880 --> 00:31:40.280
In addition to all of our
stores and physical locations,

00:31:40.280 --> 00:31:42.590
we have target.com
and our mobile apps.

00:31:42.590 --> 00:31:44.740
Now, in terms of
virtual square feet,

00:31:44.740 --> 00:31:47.020
target.com is our biggest store.

00:31:47.020 --> 00:31:49.720
It's visited by 100
million guests per month,

00:31:49.720 --> 00:31:54.910
and is currently the fourth most
visited retail site in the US.

00:31:54.910 --> 00:31:56.680
When people think
Target, a lot of people

00:31:56.680 --> 00:31:59.560
tend to think of traditional
brick and mortar retail.

00:31:59.560 --> 00:32:01.750
However, in the last
few years, we've

00:32:01.750 --> 00:32:03.460
really become a
technology company

00:32:03.460 --> 00:32:06.840
that enables our retail presence
both online and in-store.

00:32:06.840 --> 00:32:09.580
And so I'm really excited to
share some of that technology

00:32:09.580 --> 00:32:13.200
that we're using and we put
into place with you today.

00:32:13.200 --> 00:32:16.440
Where I want to start is
what Target runs on, GCP.

00:32:16.440 --> 00:32:18.580
Now, when we talk
about our website,

00:32:18.580 --> 00:32:20.190
there's a really
big component to it

00:32:20.190 --> 00:32:22.260
that we refer to
as non-commerce.

00:32:22.260 --> 00:32:26.160
And this basically is about
73% of our GCP footprint.

00:32:26.160 --> 00:32:29.680
Non-commerce is
browsing, searching,

00:32:29.680 --> 00:32:33.150
looking at product
information, images, reviews,

00:32:33.150 --> 00:32:35.440
and activities of that nature.

00:32:35.440 --> 00:32:36.660
We then have commerce.

00:32:36.660 --> 00:32:38.950
Now, commerce, as
the name implies,

00:32:38.950 --> 00:32:42.210
is adding items to your
cart, checking out,

00:32:42.210 --> 00:32:45.210
using a payment method like
a credit card, and so on.

00:32:45.210 --> 00:32:49.320
This represents about 27% of
our total footprint in GCP.

00:32:49.320 --> 00:32:50.970
And this is a segment
that is subject

00:32:50.970 --> 00:32:53.910
to regulatory compliance, so
security is extremely important

00:32:53.910 --> 00:32:55.050
here.

00:32:55.050 --> 00:32:57.780
We also have our developer
portal hosted on GCP.

00:32:57.780 --> 00:33:00.540
So this is where we host
documentation for our APIs,

00:33:00.540 --> 00:33:03.060
as well as self-service
mechanisms,

00:33:03.060 --> 00:33:05.880
to renew your API keys
and things of that nature.

00:33:05.880 --> 00:33:07.840
And we host static content.

00:33:07.840 --> 00:33:10.620
So think of JavaScript, and
images, and things like that,

00:33:10.620 --> 00:33:13.560
that we can put in GCS
and host there, and not

00:33:13.560 --> 00:33:17.430
need to run compute in order
to present that to our guests.

00:33:17.430 --> 00:33:19.560
Next what I want to
do is just run you

00:33:19.560 --> 00:33:21.450
through some very,
very high level

00:33:21.450 --> 00:33:24.360
architecture of how
we're deployed in GCP,

00:33:24.360 --> 00:33:26.050
and how things work.

00:33:26.050 --> 00:33:28.950
So this is our
non-commerce architecture.

00:33:28.950 --> 00:33:31.470
We migrated this
into GCP in 2017

00:33:31.470 --> 00:33:33.270
from another cloud provider.

00:33:33.270 --> 00:33:34.950
The entire point
of this environment

00:33:34.950 --> 00:33:38.920
is, it's a general purpose,
general tenancy environment.

00:33:38.920 --> 00:33:42.990
And we use a model that Google
refers to as standalone VPC.

00:33:42.990 --> 00:33:45.503
So we have three of these
large, monolithic projects.

00:33:45.503 --> 00:33:46.920
They're broken up
per environment.

00:33:46.920 --> 00:33:49.710
We have a lab project,
a non-prod project,

00:33:49.710 --> 00:33:50.775
and a production project.

00:33:50.775 --> 00:33:53.400
And we're I'll start with this,
if you take a look in the lower

00:33:53.400 --> 00:33:56.550
right hand corner, is our
data center connectivity.

00:33:56.550 --> 00:33:59.820
Today, we use Cloud VPN
to attach our data centers

00:33:59.820 --> 00:34:03.030
to GCP, and specifically
to a set of subnets

00:34:03.030 --> 00:34:05.780
that we refer to as
platform services.

00:34:05.780 --> 00:34:07.980
In our platform
services subnet, we

00:34:07.980 --> 00:34:10.378
operate as the name
somewhat implies, services

00:34:10.378 --> 00:34:12.420
that are common across
all of the cloud providers

00:34:12.420 --> 00:34:13.949
that we operate in.

00:34:13.949 --> 00:34:17.550
So for example, we operate
service discovery, secrets

00:34:17.550 --> 00:34:20.760
management, data persistence
through Cassandra,

00:34:20.760 --> 00:34:23.370
and message queuing via Kafka.

00:34:23.370 --> 00:34:25.260
These services can
communicate directly

00:34:25.260 --> 00:34:28.659
with our data center for
replication purposes.

00:34:28.659 --> 00:34:30.850
Up next, we have our
application hosting

00:34:30.850 --> 00:34:32.530
environment, which is on top.

00:34:32.530 --> 00:34:34.900
Now again, this is a
general tenancy environment.

00:34:34.900 --> 00:34:36.610
But what's interesting
here is, this

00:34:36.610 --> 00:34:38.659
is non routeable to
our data centers.

00:34:38.659 --> 00:34:41.350
So if our applications need
to communicate with our data

00:34:41.350 --> 00:34:43.780
centers, they actually
do so indirectly

00:34:43.780 --> 00:34:47.510
through the platform services
that we provide to them.

00:34:47.510 --> 00:34:50.480
The interesting piece of this
is this means our application

00:34:50.480 --> 00:34:53.400
hosting subnets our air
gapped from our data centers,

00:34:53.400 --> 00:34:56.550
so that's a little advantageous
in terms of security.

00:34:56.550 --> 00:34:58.477
Finally in the upper
right, what happens

00:34:58.477 --> 00:35:00.560
when we have calls that
come in from the internet,

00:35:00.560 --> 00:35:02.690
they actually
traverse CDN provider,

00:35:02.690 --> 00:35:06.300
and then go to the
appropriate load balancer.

00:35:06.300 --> 00:35:09.330
Now, next up is our
commerce architecture.

00:35:09.330 --> 00:35:12.020
We migrated this in
2018, last summer.

00:35:12.020 --> 00:35:15.030
And we migrated this
from a colo facility.

00:35:15.030 --> 00:35:18.420
This environment has
one primary purpose,

00:35:18.420 --> 00:35:20.310
and that is to
host workloads that

00:35:20.310 --> 00:35:22.500
are subject to
regulatory compliance,

00:35:22.500 --> 00:35:25.440
or have high data
security classifications.

00:35:25.440 --> 00:35:28.290
This requires really strong
isolation and segmentation

00:35:28.290 --> 00:35:30.810
between everything
that runs up there.

00:35:30.810 --> 00:35:34.210
As I change slides, you might
have noticed a subtle but very,

00:35:34.210 --> 00:35:36.390
very important difference,
and that is we're

00:35:36.390 --> 00:35:38.710
using shared VPC in
this environment.

00:35:38.710 --> 00:35:42.390
So I mentioned non-commerce
uses three monolithic projects.

00:35:42.390 --> 00:35:45.420
In commerce, we
actually use 56 projects

00:35:45.420 --> 00:35:49.950
across all of our environments
to construct our architecture.

00:35:49.950 --> 00:35:52.050
What we've done is
we've partitioned out

00:35:52.050 --> 00:35:55.080
all of our applications and
all of our platform services

00:35:55.080 --> 00:35:56.910
into their own
individual subnets,

00:35:56.910 --> 00:35:59.640
with their own individual
service projects.

00:35:59.640 --> 00:36:02.040
For data center connectivity,
we're now currently using

00:36:02.040 --> 00:36:04.980
partner interconnect, and in
another interesting divergence

00:36:04.980 --> 00:36:07.920
here, our applications
can connect directly

00:36:07.920 --> 00:36:09.632
to our data center workloads.

00:36:09.632 --> 00:36:11.340
This is particularly
important because we

00:36:11.340 --> 00:36:14.280
run some applications that need
to have synchronous callbacks

00:36:14.280 --> 00:36:16.320
to our data centers in
order to complete things

00:36:16.320 --> 00:36:19.110
like transactions, when
you're checking out.

00:36:19.110 --> 00:36:21.978
Otherwise, beyond all
of the segmentation,

00:36:21.978 --> 00:36:23.520
the platform services
that we operate

00:36:23.520 --> 00:36:25.110
are the same, and
the applications

00:36:25.110 --> 00:36:28.350
that we host are generally
operating in the same way.

00:36:28.350 --> 00:36:32.040
We use Compute Engine, and
we use HTTPS load balancers.

00:36:32.040 --> 00:36:33.750
Our ingress traffic
comes through a CDM,

00:36:33.750 --> 00:36:36.110
just like non-commerce.

00:36:36.110 --> 00:36:39.210
So let's look at a
few common pieces.

00:36:39.210 --> 00:36:40.790
The first one that
I want to touch on

00:36:40.790 --> 00:36:43.730
is the fact that we use
layer seven HTTPS load

00:36:43.730 --> 00:36:45.800
balancers in almost all cases.

00:36:45.800 --> 00:36:49.160
In fact, in over 99% of our
load balancer use cases,

00:36:49.160 --> 00:36:50.300
we use these.

00:36:50.300 --> 00:36:52.730
We use Cloud Armor
and SSL policies,

00:36:52.730 --> 00:36:57.230
which I'll touch on a little
bit later, to secure our edge.

00:36:57.230 --> 00:36:59.270
In our platform
services environment,

00:36:59.270 --> 00:37:01.550
we operate proxies and Nats.

00:37:01.550 --> 00:37:04.610
Now this is important because
our instances, by and large,

00:37:04.610 --> 00:37:06.670
don't have public
IP addresses, but we

00:37:06.670 --> 00:37:09.170
need a way for these instances
to reach out to the internet,

00:37:09.170 --> 00:37:11.120
to hit third party
APIs, to do things

00:37:11.120 --> 00:37:13.680
like address
validation for example.

00:37:13.680 --> 00:37:16.280
So we operate proxies, and Nats.

00:37:16.280 --> 00:37:18.170
And we install
inspection software that

00:37:18.170 --> 00:37:19.790
can police traffic on these.

00:37:19.790 --> 00:37:24.320
And we emit those logs to our
on premise SIEM for analysis.

00:37:24.320 --> 00:37:27.332
We use firewall rules in
all of our environments.

00:37:27.332 --> 00:37:28.790
But we take an
interesting approach

00:37:28.790 --> 00:37:30.830
with these in our
commerce environment.

00:37:30.830 --> 00:37:34.130
We use firewall rules that
are based on service accounts,

00:37:34.130 --> 00:37:36.200
as both sources and targets.

00:37:36.200 --> 00:37:39.230
And this helps us achieve a
level of micro segmentation

00:37:39.230 --> 00:37:42.540
when it comes to network
connectivity that's allowed.

00:37:42.540 --> 00:37:45.210
We use private connectivity
in all of our scenarios,

00:37:45.210 --> 00:37:47.460
and I'll touch on specifics
about private connectivity

00:37:47.460 --> 00:37:49.200
a little later on.

00:37:49.200 --> 00:37:51.030
We use Stackdriver
logging and monitoring

00:37:51.030 --> 00:37:54.450
pretty heavily, Cloud DNS
for both public and private

00:37:54.450 --> 00:37:57.210
authoritative record
resolution, and we

00:37:57.210 --> 00:38:01.230
use Cloud Pub/Sub as a way
to move traffic between GCP

00:38:01.230 --> 00:38:04.460
and our on premise data centers.

00:38:04.460 --> 00:38:06.560
So I went through all
of that to give you

00:38:06.560 --> 00:38:08.450
a little context for
these next few items

00:38:08.450 --> 00:38:11.030
that I want to discuss with you.

00:38:11.030 --> 00:38:13.250
The first one is VPC
Service Controls.

00:38:13.250 --> 00:38:15.430
Now Zach already
went through a lot

00:38:15.430 --> 00:38:18.050
of the fine details of
VPC Service Controls

00:38:18.050 --> 00:38:20.290
so I'm just going to talk
to you about a Target use

00:38:20.290 --> 00:38:21.563
case for you.

00:38:21.563 --> 00:38:23.230
What you see on the
screen is a grouping

00:38:23.230 --> 00:38:24.760
of our commerce projects.

00:38:24.760 --> 00:38:27.550
And you also see some of
our non-commerce projects.

00:38:27.550 --> 00:38:30.250
Now, we basically have
dozens of projects,

00:38:30.250 --> 00:38:32.860
and these have varying different
levels of data security

00:38:32.860 --> 00:38:34.240
classifications.

00:38:34.240 --> 00:38:37.300
They also may have varying
levels of regulatory compliance

00:38:37.300 --> 00:38:38.840
that are required.

00:38:38.840 --> 00:38:41.170
What we don't want to
do in these situations

00:38:41.170 --> 00:38:43.140
are cross logical boundaries.

00:38:43.140 --> 00:38:46.780
But the problem is,
earlier on, last year,

00:38:46.780 --> 00:38:48.970
these were more
imaginary boundaries,

00:38:48.970 --> 00:38:52.360
defined by Target policy,
and not anything concrete.

00:38:52.360 --> 00:38:55.930
So for example, if you had the
appropriate service account

00:38:55.930 --> 00:38:58.600
credentials, you
could, for example,

00:38:58.600 --> 00:39:00.070
reach from our
commerce environment

00:39:00.070 --> 00:39:01.810
into our non-commerce
environment,

00:39:01.810 --> 00:39:05.330
look at some stuff that's in the
storage there, or vise versa.

00:39:05.330 --> 00:39:07.390
Now we don't allow
this by policy,

00:39:07.390 --> 00:39:09.830
but there isn't an actual
way to prevent this.

00:39:09.830 --> 00:39:13.880
So what can we do to prevent
this type of activity?

00:39:13.880 --> 00:39:16.480
Well, we can use VPC
Service Controls,

00:39:16.480 --> 00:39:19.030
and we can draw a perimeter
around the projects

00:39:19.030 --> 00:39:21.280
that all have the same
data classification.

00:39:21.280 --> 00:39:25.480
So in this example, we draw this
around our commerce project.

00:39:25.480 --> 00:39:28.360
What we do then is
we disable API access

00:39:28.360 --> 00:39:30.140
that crosses this perimeter.

00:39:30.140 --> 00:39:33.010
So this denies the access across
the perimeter, both inside

00:39:33.010 --> 00:39:36.700
and out, and helps us prevent
crossing between different data

00:39:36.700 --> 00:39:38.990
classifications.

00:39:38.990 --> 00:39:42.290
There's another really big
benefit here, and that is,

00:39:42.290 --> 00:39:45.550
what happens if a third party
suddenly becomes involved,

00:39:45.550 --> 00:39:47.860
and you're worried
about data exfiltration?

00:39:47.860 --> 00:39:51.610
Now, this is basically a
nightmare scenario for you.

00:39:51.610 --> 00:39:53.650
You don't want people
to exfiltrate data.

00:39:53.650 --> 00:39:56.290
And the problem is, once
data has been exfiltrated,

00:39:56.290 --> 00:39:58.690
you really can't put the
genie back into the bottle.

00:39:58.690 --> 00:40:02.230
So data breaches, and data
loss, are a huge concern,

00:40:02.230 --> 00:40:04.960
and you don't want to lose
the trust of your customers.

00:40:04.960 --> 00:40:07.210
Thankfully, VPC
Service Controls also

00:40:07.210 --> 00:40:09.250
applies to this
particular scenario,

00:40:09.250 --> 00:40:13.360
so exfiltration
attempts can be blocked.

00:40:13.360 --> 00:40:15.570
So just some key takeaways here.

00:40:15.570 --> 00:40:17.200
The VPC Service
Controls basically

00:40:17.200 --> 00:40:20.170
helps prevent forms of
unauthorized data movement,

00:40:20.170 --> 00:40:23.530
and that applies, regardless
of if you control the projects,

00:40:23.530 --> 00:40:24.970
or not.

00:40:24.970 --> 00:40:27.310
The key here is this
helps you prevent

00:40:27.310 --> 00:40:30.110
that activity from occurring
in the first place.

00:40:30.110 --> 00:40:33.580
Now at Target, we have ways
to detect data exfiltration.

00:40:33.580 --> 00:40:36.460
But once you detect that
data has been exfiltrated,

00:40:36.460 --> 00:40:37.690
it's a little too late.

00:40:37.690 --> 00:40:40.300
So with a combination
of VPC Service Controls

00:40:40.300 --> 00:40:43.240
and our detective methods,
we feel really comfortable

00:40:43.240 --> 00:40:44.960
with our security posture.

00:40:44.960 --> 00:40:48.730
And the great part is, this
works, regardless of who

00:40:48.730 --> 00:40:50.725
or what it's trying
to move this data.

00:40:50.725 --> 00:40:52.600
So whether you're trying
to do it on purpose,

00:40:52.600 --> 00:40:54.850
or maybe take a little
shortcut at work because you're

00:40:54.850 --> 00:40:58.685
about to approach a
deadline, it'll still work.

00:40:58.685 --> 00:41:01.060
Next I want to talk to you
about the interconnect options

00:41:01.060 --> 00:41:02.120
that we use.

00:41:02.120 --> 00:41:04.090
Now I'm going to
start with Cloud VPN,

00:41:04.090 --> 00:41:07.240
because when we
migrated to GCP in 2017,

00:41:07.240 --> 00:41:09.400
Cloud VPN was the first
interconnect mechanism

00:41:09.400 --> 00:41:10.430
that we've used.

00:41:10.430 --> 00:41:12.910
So we've been using it
for over two years now.

00:41:12.910 --> 00:41:14.770
Now the great part
about Cloud VPN

00:41:14.770 --> 00:41:16.372
is it's relatively inexpensive.

00:41:16.372 --> 00:41:17.830
It's pretty quick
to set up, and it

00:41:17.830 --> 00:41:20.298
can scale to some pretty
reasonable performance.

00:41:20.298 --> 00:41:21.340
There are some drawbacks.

00:41:21.340 --> 00:41:23.080
If you have really
high throughput needs,

00:41:23.080 --> 00:41:26.440
or if you have extremely
high demands for reliability,

00:41:26.440 --> 00:41:28.620
but by and large it works great.

00:41:28.620 --> 00:41:31.870
Now importantly,
VPN will typically

00:41:31.870 --> 00:41:33.700
traverse the public internet.

00:41:33.700 --> 00:41:35.950
And that means that there
can be some issues that will

00:41:35.950 --> 00:41:38.110
impact your VPN connectivity.

00:41:38.110 --> 00:41:41.200
So for example, let's say
that the red provider here

00:41:41.200 --> 00:41:43.090
has some sort of
issue with their edge,

00:41:43.090 --> 00:41:44.450
and their routes are withdrawn.

00:41:44.450 --> 00:41:48.110
Now this can cause a bit of
temporary connectivity issues

00:41:48.110 --> 00:41:50.840
if your traffic was pathed
over the red provider.

00:41:50.840 --> 00:41:54.010
You can also have issues
upstream or downstream,

00:41:54.010 --> 00:41:57.700
with capacity, because the
provider withdrew their routes.

00:41:57.700 --> 00:42:00.970
And finally, there's a scenario
that occurs sometimes a little

00:42:00.970 --> 00:42:03.460
too often, and these
are route hijacks.

00:42:03.460 --> 00:42:05.920
Now these don't necessarily
have to be malicious,

00:42:05.920 --> 00:42:09.130
but they can still impact the
stability of your VPN tunnels.

00:42:09.130 --> 00:42:13.127
So all said, cloud VPN actually
works pretty well for us

00:42:13.127 --> 00:42:14.710
in non-commerce,
because we don't have

00:42:14.710 --> 00:42:16.760
any synchronous requirements.

00:42:16.760 --> 00:42:21.130
However, we do need to
have higher availability

00:42:21.130 --> 00:42:22.730
in the commerce environment.

00:42:22.730 --> 00:42:24.860
And that is because we
have synchronous callbacks

00:42:24.860 --> 00:42:26.260
for our data centers.

00:42:26.260 --> 00:42:28.810
Any interruptions
in those callbacks

00:42:28.810 --> 00:42:30.880
prevents checkout
from working properly,

00:42:30.880 --> 00:42:32.480
which impacts our revenue.

00:42:32.480 --> 00:42:35.170
So we wanted to explore
a different solution.

00:42:35.170 --> 00:42:37.120
We moved to partner
interconnect.

00:42:37.120 --> 00:42:40.690
And what we do here is we
operate a router at our edge.

00:42:40.690 --> 00:42:42.490
Our router connects
to an ISP, who

00:42:42.490 --> 00:42:45.490
happens to be one of the
partner interconnect providers,

00:42:45.490 --> 00:42:48.640
and we have a private, redundant
path that avoids the internet,

00:42:48.640 --> 00:42:50.770
and meets back up with
Google at its peering edge,

00:42:50.770 --> 00:42:52.660
and goes into the VPC.

00:42:52.660 --> 00:42:55.120
So this is really
helpful, in terms

00:42:55.120 --> 00:42:59.050
of maintaining a very, very
high stability environment

00:42:59.050 --> 00:43:01.210
for critical connectivity.

00:43:01.210 --> 00:43:03.220
As Shubhikia mentioned,
it's scalable as well,

00:43:03.220 --> 00:43:05.500
so you can scale between
50 megabits per second

00:43:05.500 --> 00:43:07.457
to 10 gigabits per second.

00:43:07.457 --> 00:43:09.790
And if you're looking to
provision things really quickly

00:43:09.790 --> 00:43:11.320
and you have high
bandwidth needs,

00:43:11.320 --> 00:43:13.570
you can actually provision this
a little quicker than direct

00:43:13.570 --> 00:43:14.695
interconnect in some cases.

00:43:17.260 --> 00:43:19.540
Now just briefly I want
to talk about Cloud Armor,

00:43:19.540 --> 00:43:21.220
and what I have
here on the screen

00:43:21.220 --> 00:43:23.650
is an example flow
from our guests,

00:43:23.650 --> 00:43:27.250
calling all the way into some
resources that we have in GCP.

00:43:27.250 --> 00:43:30.160
So what happens here
is our guest will

00:43:30.160 --> 00:43:32.980
call to one of our
digital properties,

00:43:32.980 --> 00:43:35.890
and that call will go
through our CDM, Fastly.

00:43:35.890 --> 00:43:38.200
What will happen here is
we'll traverse the CDM.

00:43:38.200 --> 00:43:40.060
There's a number of
things that happen here.

00:43:40.060 --> 00:43:42.640
Web application firewalls
or WAFs, routing,

00:43:42.640 --> 00:43:43.915
caching, and so on.

00:43:43.915 --> 00:43:45.790
And eventually that
request will make it down

00:43:45.790 --> 00:43:48.400
to our load balancers,
through Cloud Armor,

00:43:48.400 --> 00:43:50.500
and into the backend instance.

00:43:50.500 --> 00:43:52.750
Once that traffics there,
we'll generate a response,

00:43:52.750 --> 00:43:55.270
and it'll go all the way
back out to our guest.

00:43:55.270 --> 00:43:57.610
Now the reason
this works is we've

00:43:57.610 --> 00:43:59.410
configured Cloud
Armor to allow traffic

00:43:59.410 --> 00:44:03.310
in from Fastly, and a few
other approved sources.

00:44:03.310 --> 00:44:06.730
Our guests, though,
aren't the only thing that

00:44:06.730 --> 00:44:07.990
are out there on the internet.

00:44:07.990 --> 00:44:10.750
So you can imagine there's
some malicious actors out there

00:44:10.750 --> 00:44:13.000
running port scanners and
various other reconnaissance

00:44:13.000 --> 00:44:13.820
things.

00:44:13.820 --> 00:44:16.610
And we don't actually want
them to hit our services,

00:44:16.610 --> 00:44:18.860
and they really don't need
to in the first place.

00:44:18.860 --> 00:44:22.650
In fact, we only want Fastly,
our CDN, our data centers,

00:44:22.650 --> 00:44:25.150
and our headquarters locations,
to be able to hit these load

00:44:25.150 --> 00:44:26.300
balancers.

00:44:26.300 --> 00:44:29.230
So when they send traffic,
it hits the load balancer,

00:44:29.230 --> 00:44:30.730
and we're actually
able to see this.

00:44:30.730 --> 00:44:32.260
It's logged in Stackdriver.

00:44:32.260 --> 00:44:35.590
But the traffic is blocked
by a Cloud Armor policy,

00:44:35.590 --> 00:44:38.890
and those requests never make
it to the backend instances.

00:44:38.890 --> 00:44:40.780
In either case,
we see these logs,

00:44:40.780 --> 00:44:45.900
and we can export them to our
SIEM for security analysis.

00:44:45.900 --> 00:44:46.410
All right.

00:44:46.410 --> 00:44:48.060
Next up, really
quickly, I just want

00:44:48.060 --> 00:44:51.180
to talk about our use cases
for VPC Flow Logs and firewall

00:44:51.180 --> 00:44:52.410
logs.

00:44:52.410 --> 00:44:54.250
Zach did a great job
running through these.

00:44:54.250 --> 00:44:57.570
There's a lot of rich
annotations and metadata

00:44:57.570 --> 00:44:59.350
that you get with these.

00:44:59.350 --> 00:45:02.220
But what I thought I'd do is
show you a sanitized firewall

00:45:02.220 --> 00:45:04.230
log example, in case
you haven't seen

00:45:04.230 --> 00:45:07.270
one of these before, and just
talk through it a little bit.

00:45:07.270 --> 00:45:09.990
So what we can see in
this particular example

00:45:09.990 --> 00:45:12.810
is we have an instance that's
running a service project,

00:45:12.810 --> 00:45:15.240
and it seems to be
a little suspicious.

00:45:15.240 --> 00:45:16.830
And notably what we
can see happening

00:45:16.830 --> 00:45:19.020
here is this instance,
is connecting

00:45:19.020 --> 00:45:21.640
to an IP address that's
out on the public internet.

00:45:21.640 --> 00:45:28.110
And it's using protocol 17 in
port 53, so this is UDP 53 DNS.

00:45:28.110 --> 00:45:32.310
Now in situations
like this, DNS could

00:45:32.310 --> 00:45:34.890
be used as a command and
control mechanism for a botnet,

00:45:34.890 --> 00:45:35.950
for example.

00:45:35.950 --> 00:45:37.680
So this isn't really great.

00:45:37.680 --> 00:45:40.650
Now the good news is, we've
actually blocked this traffic

00:45:40.650 --> 00:45:42.660
with an explicit
egress denial rule

00:45:42.660 --> 00:45:44.770
that we've set at a
really low priority.

00:45:44.770 --> 00:45:47.400
So we have some other
rules that mandate

00:45:47.400 --> 00:45:51.340
having an allow rule in place
before that traffic can egress,

00:45:51.340 --> 00:45:52.500
so we're protected here.

00:45:52.500 --> 00:45:54.570
But it's really
helpful to know this.

00:45:54.570 --> 00:45:56.370
We take this
information, and we use

00:45:56.370 --> 00:46:00.210
Cloud Pub/Sub to send it to
our on-prem SIEM for analysis

00:46:00.210 --> 00:46:01.470
and alerting.

00:46:01.470 --> 00:46:05.970
We also can use other syncs and
exports, like BigQuery or GCS,

00:46:05.970 --> 00:46:09.150
and we actually export these
to GCS, as well, for long term

00:46:09.150 --> 00:46:10.650
retention, and in
case we need to do

00:46:10.650 --> 00:46:13.643
any sort of forensic analysis.

00:46:13.643 --> 00:46:15.060
So looking forward
really quickly,

00:46:15.060 --> 00:46:17.910
I just want to run through
a couple of things.

00:46:17.910 --> 00:46:19.590
Over the next year,
Target is really

00:46:19.590 --> 00:46:21.900
looking forward to some
enhancements with VPC Service

00:46:21.900 --> 00:46:22.557
Controls.

00:46:22.557 --> 00:46:24.390
There's a piece that
we didn't touch on yet,

00:46:24.390 --> 00:46:27.060
called
restricted.GoogleAPIs.com.

00:46:27.060 --> 00:46:29.140
It's a /30 net block.

00:46:29.140 --> 00:46:31.230
Now the real advantage
to using this,

00:46:31.230 --> 00:46:34.260
beyond being able to restrict
what APIs you can actually talk

00:46:34.260 --> 00:46:37.530
to, is it's a /30 net block.

00:46:37.530 --> 00:46:39.120
So it's a much
smaller address space

00:46:39.120 --> 00:46:41.580
to manage than the
19 net blocks that

00:46:41.580 --> 00:46:44.130
represent Google's public APIs.

00:46:44.130 --> 00:46:47.880
So it ultimately results in
a reduction in firewall rules

00:46:47.880 --> 00:46:51.870
and routes, and other
various management overhead.

00:46:51.870 --> 00:46:53.010
Cloud NAT.

00:46:53.010 --> 00:46:55.350
Now managing NATs is toilsome.

00:46:55.350 --> 00:46:57.705
We do it, and they can
be difficult to scale.

00:46:57.705 --> 00:46:59.580
So we're really looking
forward to Cloud NAT.

00:46:59.580 --> 00:47:03.480
But today we install software on
our NATs that inspects traffic,

00:47:03.480 --> 00:47:05.280
and that isn't available
yet in Cloud NAT.

00:47:05.280 --> 00:47:08.820
So we're looking forward to some
potential developments there.

00:47:08.820 --> 00:47:12.090
And then finally, Google's
managed TLS certificates.

00:47:12.090 --> 00:47:15.900
Target operates over 200
HTTPS load balancers,

00:47:15.900 --> 00:47:18.550
and that is a lot of
certificates to keep track of.

00:47:18.550 --> 00:47:20.250
So we're going to
have some discussions

00:47:20.250 --> 00:47:23.130
with our internal security
teams later this year,

00:47:23.130 --> 00:47:26.040
and see if we can move to these.

00:47:26.040 --> 00:47:28.710
Now before we move
to Q&amp;A, I just

00:47:28.710 --> 00:47:30.270
wanted to give a shameless plug.

00:47:30.270 --> 00:47:31.800
A colleague, Nicole,
and I, will be

00:47:31.800 --> 00:47:33.840
giving a presentation
tomorrow that

00:47:33.840 --> 00:47:36.150
discusses a little
more in detail

00:47:36.150 --> 00:47:39.870
some of the architecture,
specifically around shared VPC,

00:47:39.870 --> 00:47:43.265
VPC peering, and firewall rules
that involve service accounts.

00:47:43.265 --> 00:47:44.640
So if you're
interested in seeing

00:47:44.640 --> 00:47:47.100
this in a little more detail,
please join us tomorrow.

00:47:47.100 --> 00:47:49.180
It should be a lot of fun.

00:47:49.180 --> 00:47:51.310
And I know that
conferences are busy,

00:47:51.310 --> 00:47:53.360
so we're going to do Q&amp;A
in a little bit here.

00:47:53.360 --> 00:47:55.690
If you have any
Target-specific questions

00:47:55.690 --> 00:47:57.462
and you need to run
to your next session,

00:47:57.462 --> 00:47:59.670
this email address will be
active for the next couple

00:47:59.670 --> 00:48:00.230
weeks.

00:48:00.230 --> 00:48:02.140
So feel free to shoot
me an email address,

00:48:02.140 --> 00:48:04.960
or shoot me an email, and I'll
try to answer it as best I can.

00:48:04.960 --> 00:48:07.210
And with that, I'll hand
things back over to Shubhikia

00:48:07.210 --> 00:48:08.690
to wrap things up.

00:48:08.690 --> 00:48:09.190
Thank you.

